WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:05.880
"John Wick: Chapter 4" might just be the biggest&nbsp;
and best "Wick" yet, but as the old saying goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.775
you can't make an epic action movie with cutting&nbsp;
some heads open. We'll let Keanu Reeves explain.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.940
The "John Wick" franchise has been&nbsp;
praised for its use of practical effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:18.720
and immaculate stunt-work, always pushing&nbsp;
the boundaries of what audiences expect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.340
with each entry being bigger,&nbsp;
bolder, and arguably better.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:27.060
Naturally, this makes the production of the movies&nbsp;
a highly choreographed affair behind the scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:31.320
on account of the intense moments of&nbsp;
hand-to-hand combat, snappy gunplay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:37.200
and jaw-dropping stunts. Unfortunately, accidents&nbsp;
do happen, and it seems as if Keanu Reeves himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.589
is responsible for injuring a co-worker during&nbsp;
the production of "John Wick: Chapter 4."

00:00:41.589 --> 00:00:45.180
During an interview with ComicBook.com,&nbsp;
Reeves was asked about the number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.940
accidents that occur during the&nbsp;
filming of a "John Wick" movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.929
and he prefaced his answer by describing&nbsp;
the conditions on set. Reeves said,

00:00:51.929 --> 00:00:56.040
"Everyone is pretty tired, but it's the best&nbsp;
kind of tired. It's like you climbed a mountain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.980
now you're resting; like you&nbsp;
finished the game and you won."

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.726
Reeves then mentioned two on-set accidents&nbsp;
that sent stunt performers to the hospital:

00:01:03.726 --> 00:01:09.660
"In terms of accidents, I made a mistake&nbsp;
once and I cut a gentleman's head open. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.180
that really f-----g sucked. Excuse my language."

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.800
The other incident involved one of the&nbsp;
film's many car-based stunts. Reeves added,

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.060
"One guy got hit by a car. He was in the car, so&nbsp;
he had to go to the hospital, but he was okay."

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:25.320
Reeves didn't provide any context to&nbsp;
the circumstances of either incident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.600
and considering the nature&nbsp;
of the "John Wick" movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.900
it could have been anybody in "Chapter&nbsp;
4" who happens to come up against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:35.879
fearsome killer. And "Chapter 4" is filled&nbsp;
to the brim with chaotic vehicular action.

00:01:35.879 --> 00:01:40.740
In an interview with Digital Trends, stunt&nbsp;
coordinators Scott Rogers and Stephen Dunlevy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:45.000
opened up about how difficult it was to film&nbsp;
the Paris Arc de Triomphe sequence because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.592
of the amount of people that needed&nbsp;
to get hit with cars. Rogers said,

00:01:48.592 --> 00:01:50.460
"[We] had to figure out how many people can you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:53.760
hit with cars without taking each one&nbsp;
of them to the hospital. We want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:57.467
do these spectacular stunts but without&nbsp;
losing sight of the reality of it all."

00:01:57.467 --> 00:02:00.360
Safety is critical to making&nbsp;
these action sequences work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.620
and Reeves told Comicbook.com that the "John&nbsp;
Wick" team really cares for one another, adding,

00:02:04.908 --> 00:02:08.988
"All of this is to say, some of the stuff&nbsp;
that we're doing, you do have to take care.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.800
It's great that everyone is&nbsp;
watching out for each other."

00:02:14.057 --> 00:02:18.154
Considering that there are 98 people&nbsp;
credited for stunts on "Chapter 4,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.154 --> 00:02:22.080
it seems like the movie had a veritable&nbsp;
army of those willing to fight, shoot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:26.700
and get hit for the sake of cinematic glory,&nbsp;
despite the risk of accidents. Then again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:30.729
making something great is hardly ever&nbsp;
easy or without the occasional misstep.

00:02:30.729 --> 00:02:34.860
Those in the stunt industry love working on&nbsp;
the "John Wick" flicks as much as audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.180
enjoy watching them. Continuing his chat with&nbsp;
Digital Trends, "Chapter 4" stunt coordinator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:43.980
Stephen Dunlevy described the franchise as "the&nbsp;
pinnacle of the stunt industry" and revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:48.600
that he turned down other films to work on the&nbsp;
latest offering from director Chad Stahelski.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.900
Despite the injuries that manifested on&nbsp;
the film's set, it's clear that the "John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.452
Wick" films value personal safety and&nbsp;
pushing the boundaries of stunt work.

00:02:55.452 --> 00:02:59.040
As it stands, "John Wick: Chapter 4"&nbsp;
is the most critically-acclaimed film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.320
in the franchise, with many&nbsp;
claiming it's one of the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:05.280
action films ever put to screen.&nbsp;
Clearly, hard work does pay off.

